\title{Testing whether a task can test an architecture hypothesis}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Architecture comparisons assume the benchmark can \emph{test} the hypothesis: that the task
contains the structure the architecture is meant to exploit. When it does not, a null is
uninformative and a single-seed positive is unfalsifiable noise. Yet both are routinely
reported as evidence. We give a \textbf{pre-registered protocol for deciding whether a task
can test an architecture hypothesis at all}, before trusting any comparison run on it: state
the structural precondition; build a graded manipulation ladder targeting it; gate each rung
for learnability with one seed; promote only load-bearing rungs to multi-seed evaluation; and
attribute any induced hardness to the precondition rather than a confound before comparing
architectures. Instantiated on a claim from our own earlier, unpublished work, that an
inverted actor--critic asymmetry improves sparse-reward navigation, the protocol first finds
the single-seed advantage does not replicate (IQM $27.56$ vs $27.66$, overlapping 95\% CIs,
$P=0.25$), then shows \emph{why} the question cannot be settled on this task at all: no
degradation of the observation reaches the required regime. Every rung either
\textbf{ceilings}, \textbf{fractures} into a give-up local optimum, or \textbf{fragments}
across seed-selected local optima. We report this as a worked negative outcome of the
protocol, not as evidence against asymmetry, and specify what a task that could arbitrate the
hypothesis would need.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The reproducibility literature has taught deep reinforcement learning to distrust a single
run. Seed variance can flip a conclusion, and much reported progress has not survived
multi-seed re-evaluation \citep{henderson2018matters,agarwal2021precipice}; the accepted
remedy is to report interquartile means (IQM) with stratified-bootstrap confidence intervals.
This closes one failure mode, mistaking noise for signal, but leaves a prior one untouched. A
comparison can be statistically impeccable and still meaningless: if the benchmark does not
contain the structure the architecture is meant to exploit, multi-seed statistics measure,
with great precision, an effect the task was never capable of showing. The two cases are
observationally identical from inside the experiment. A null on a task where the effect is
genuinely absent, and a null on a task that could never have expressed it, produce the same
numbers. rliable can tell you a comparison is statistically sound; it cannot tell you it is
meaningful. Pre-registration can tell you that you did not fish for the result; it cannot tell
you the pond contains fish.

The gap has a common structure. An architecture hypothesis almost always asserts that
\emph{architectural property $A$ helps because it suits some structure $S$ of the problem}:
inverted actor--critic asymmetry suits a policy that is hard to represent, convolution suits
spatial locality, attention suits long-range dependency. Such a hypothesis is only testable
on a task that actually contains $S$. Where $S$ is absent, $A$ has nothing to act on, so the
hypothesis predicts no effect \emph{by construction}: a null is uninformative and any
single-seed win is noise dressed as evidence. Nothing in a standard train-and-evaluate loop
flags this. The task fails to pose the question, and the experiment answers a different one,
both without visible symptom.

We arrived at this through a concrete claim of our own. In earlier, unpublished experiments
of ours, an inverted actor--critic asymmetry, a policy network deeper than its value network,
appeared to beat a symmetric baseline at matched budget on a sparse-reward indoor-navigation
task, on a single seed. The claim comes apart on re-examination, but \emph{how} it comes
apart is the useful observation: it asked a question the task could not answer.

This paper contributes \textbf{a reusable protocol} (\S\ref{sec:protocol}) for deciding
whether a task can test a given architecture hypothesis before trusting any comparison run on
it, and \textbf{a worked negative outcome}: applied to the navigation task, no pre-registered
degradation induces the regime the hypothesis needs. Throughout, this is a statement about
\emph{the task}, not the hypothesis. We show this task class cannot test inverted asymmetry,
and specify (\S\ref{sec:need}) what a task that could would need, not that asymmetry fails in
reinforcement learning.

\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Reproducibility and evaluation.} Deep RL results are highly sensitive to seeds,
implementation details, and hyperparameters, and single-run comparisons frequently fail to
replicate \citep{henderson2018matters}; the standard response is distributional statistics
rather than point estimates \citep{agarwal2021precipice}. We adopt this machinery throughout.
Our concern is orthogonal: these methods establish whether a measured difference is
\emph{statistically} real, not whether the task could have produced it at all.

\paragraph{Construct validity.} That benchmarks may not measure what they are taken to
measure is not new. Under the banner of \emph{construct validity}, originating in
psychological measurement theory \citep{cronbach1955construct} and recently imported into
machine-learning evaluation critique \citep{raji2021everything}, recent work develops explicit
validity conditions for predictive benchmarking \citep{freiesleben2025benchmarking} and
documents pervasive weaknesses across hundreds of benchmarks \citep{bean2025measuring}. That
literature is almost entirely \emph{diagnostic}: it reviews existing benchmarks and names how
their validity fails. What it does not provide is a constructive, executable procedure a
researcher can run \emph{before} trusting a particular comparison. Our contribution is exactly
that instrument, for one sharply-defined validity question, with a pre-registered, pass/fail
outcome.

\paragraph{Asymmetric actor--critic.} The assumption that actor and critic share topology and
capacity has been questioned from more than one direction. One line studies the
\emph{small-actor} regime, where shrinking the actor degrades performance through value
underestimation \citep{mastikhina2025optimistic}. A different sense of asymmetry gives the
critic privileged state while the actor sees only observations \citep{pinto2018asymmetric},
an asymmetry of \emph{information} rather than capacity. We examine capacity in the direction
opposite the small-actor line. That capacity asymmetry \emph{can} produce pronounced effects
on tasks containing the relevant structure sharpens rather than undercuts our claim: whether a
\emph{given} task can reveal it must itself be tested.

\section{Results: from re-evaluation to task validity}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Phase 1: the reported effect does not replicate}
\label{sec:phase1}

The environment is a $20\times20$ residential grid (\texttt{Discrete(5)} actions, a 16-D
observation, reward $R(L)=30-0.2L$ for an $L$-step success), sparse in the sense that reward
arrives only on reaching the target room, a setting long recognised as hard because
uninformative early experience gives almost no gradient to follow
\citep{andrychowicz2017her}. Holding the original single-seed claim to a 10-seed standard
with IQM and 95\% stratified-bootstrap CIs \citep{agarwal2021precipice}, across four PPO
\citep{schulman2017ppo} capacity ratios at fixed budget, the advantage disappears: IQM
$\mathbf{27.56}$ (inverted) versus $\mathbf{27.66}$ (symmetric), overlapping CIs, and
probability of improvement $P(\text{inverted}>\text{symmetric})=\mathbf{0.25}$. A companion
claim of ${\approx}12.5\times$ PPO-over-DQN sample efficiency dissolves the same way once
measured in environment steps rather than episodes: the two families' step-to-90\%
distributions overlap and the slowest configuration is a PPO one
(Appendix~\ref{app:phase1}). The primary finding is that the effect does not replicate, but a
null here is not yet an answer. The 16-D observation hands the agent its position and
distance-to-target, so the optimal policy is close to a smooth distance-descent that a shallow
network represents as well as a deep one: the precondition the hypothesis needs is unmet
\emph{by construction}, and a null is exactly what the theory predicts.

\subsection{Phase 2: a pre-registered ladder shows the task cannot test the hypothesis}
\label{sec:phase2}

Can \textbf{any} degradation of the observation put the task into the regime the hypothesis
needs, a policy genuinely hard to represent while the value stays smooth, without simply
making the task unlearnable? We answer with a \textbf{pre-registered} experiment: design,
hypotheses, falsification criteria, and per-outcome interpretations were frozen before any
run, and a dated amendment trail records every subsequent decision. The independent variable
is a ladder of observation wrappers over the frozen environment, monotone in policy-relevant
information removed; the primary endpoint is the inverted$-$symmetric IQM gap. Because a
floor effect would masquerade as the target regime, each rung is first single-seed-gated for
learnability before any load-bearing cell is promoted to 10 seeds. Guided by the
POMDP-difficulty literature, in which static feature removal is often insufficient and
temporal hidden state is what makes policies hard \citep{hausknecht2015drqn,morad2023popgym},
we test four mechanisms: \textbf{pure removal} (drop global position, then
distance-to-target); \textbf{flicker $+$ frame-stack}, masking each frame with probability $p$
and stacking the last $k=4$ \citep{mnih2015human}, so any hardness remains representable by a
feedforward policy rather than requiring recurrence \citep{kapturowski2019r2d2};
\textbf{targeted aliasing} (drop the region one-hot, so room, hallway, and doorway cells are
indistinguishable from a single frame); and \textbf{proximity noise} (flip each proximity bit
with probability $q=0.3$).

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Phase 2 claim-grade ladder cells: symmetric PPO, 10 seeds per cell, rliable IQM $+$
95\% CI. Per-room SR is the mean across seeds of each seed's own success fraction.
``Pat.'' counts distinct per-seed (kitchen, bedroom, bathroom) SR combinations across the ten
seeds: 1 means every seed reached the same behavioural regime, a large count means the seeds
scattered. Single-seed learnability gates are calibration and appear nowhere here.}
\label{tab:ladder}
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llllll}
\toprule
Cell & obs / steps & IQM eval return (95\% CI) & per-room SR & pat. & regime \\
\midrule
A-STRICT & 13-D / 200k & 27.24 [27.21, 27.27] & 1.00/1.00/1.00 & 1/10 & ceiling \\
Aliasing & 10-D / 200k & 27.17 [27.04, 27.37] & 1.00/1.00/1.00 & 1/10 & ceiling \\
Prox-noise $q{=}0.3$ & 13-D / 200k & 26.78 [26.41, 26.93] & 1.00/1.00/1.00 & 1/10 & ceiling \\
Flicker $p{=}0.7$ & 52-D / 500k & 26.14 [25.41, 26.32] & 1.00/1.00/0.93 & 2/10 & ceiling (slow) \\
Flicker $p{=}0.8$ & 52-D / 200k & \phantom{0}8.95 [\phantom{0}6.43, 13.01] & 0.00/1.00/0.33 & 3/10 & \textbf{fracture} \\
