\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.70\linewidth]{p2_ladder_iqm.png}
\caption{Table~\ref{tab:ladder} plotted: per-cell IQM eval return $+$ 95\% CI. Four ceilings
cluster near the 27.8 optimal-path reference; the $p=0.8$ fracture sits at 8.95 (200k) and its
matched-budget fragmentation at 13.24 (500k), whose interval overlaps the fracture's. The
overlap is the point: the two budgets are indistinguishable in mean return, and differ only in
per-seed distribution.}
\label{fig:ladder}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:ladder} reports every claim-grade cell. Three readings are decisive.
\textbf{First, the ceilings are real ceilings}, not slow learners or wall-avoidance floors:
the 200k ceiling cells reach 100\% success on all three rooms with tight CIs, proximity noise
does so at a collision rate of $0.00$, and even the hardest \emph{learnable} flicker
($p=0.7$) reaches the ceiling given budget, with 9/10 seeds at full success.

\textbf{Second, the one cell that leaves the ceiling does not become hard-but-learnable; it
fragments, and the two budgets together are the finding.} At 200k, $p=0.8$ looked like a clean
systematic fracture: IQM collapsed to $8.95$, the kitchen was abandoned in 10/10 seeds and the
bedroom solved in 10/10, failures were timeouts rather than collisions, and only three
distinct per-room outcome patterns appeared. Re-run at matched budget ($10$ seeds $\times$
500k, for parity with the $p=0.7$ cell), the aggregate barely moves, $13.24$ against $8.95$
with substantially overlapping intervals, so mean return is not statistically distinguishable
between budgets. The \emph{distribution}, however, changes completely: six distinct per-room
patterns now appear. The kitchen, abandoned by every seed at 200k, is solved by five of ten;
conversely two seeds abandon the \emph{bedroom}, the one room every seed solved at 200k. Only
two of ten seeds solve all three rooms. The 200k reading, ``systematic near/far give-up'',
does not survive the matched budget. What both budgets together support is stronger: $p=0.8$
places the task in a regime of \textbf{landscape fragmentation}, where multiple
locally-optimal \emph{reactive} policies partition the outcome space by room and seed
selection determines which one a run finds. Read down the ladder this is a graded trend, not
an isolated cell: distinct per-seed patterns rise monotonically with degradation severity,
$1/1/1/2/3/6$ across the six cells. Either way the rung is disqualified for an architecture
comparison, and for the same reason: a gap measured on it would reflect which architecture
more often lands in which basin, not whether $A$ suits $S$.

\textbf{Third, this exhausts the axis.} Across removal, flicker, aliasing, and sensor noise,
the pre-registered set of single-map observation degradations, no rung meets the precondition.
The pre-registered primary endpoint is defined only on a rung passing the attribution guard of
step~5 below, and no rung did. The gap was therefore never computed on a qualifying cell, and
Table~\ref{tab:ladder} is symmetric-only for exactly that reason: the non-computation
\emph{is} the result. Why the task resists every manipulation, namely that its observation is
over-determined, its optimal policy reactive, and its single layout memorisable, is analysed
in Appendix~\ref{app:mechanism}. Every aggregate reported here regenerates from the released
per-seed CSVs under a seeded bootstrap (Appendix~\ref{app:repro}).

\section{The protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

The navigation study instantiates a problem that recurs whenever architectures are compared
empirically. We distil it into a reusable procedure, stated in task- and hypothesis-agnostic
terms.

\begin{center}
\fbox{\parbox{0.955\linewidth}{
\textbf{Can this task test this architecture hypothesis?} \emph{Given ``property $A$ helps
because it suits problem structure $S$'':}
\begin{enumerate}\setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}\setlength{\parskip}{0pt}\setlength{\topsep}{2pt}
\item \textbf{State the precondition.} Write down $S$ explicitly, and the observable signature
  of $S$ present versus absent. If you cannot name $S$, you cannot test the claim.
\item \textbf{Design a manipulation ladder.} Build graded task variants that monotonically
  vary the presence of $S$, holding reward, dynamics, budget, and optimiser fixed.
\item \textbf{Gate each rung for learnability: one seed, cheap.} Confirm a \emph{standard}
  agent can still learn each rung before spending statistical compute. Discard rungs where the
  baseline floors: an unlearnable rung looks ``hard'' but tests nothing.
\item \textbf{Promote load-bearing rungs to multi-seed.} For rungs a conclusion rests on, run
  enough seeds for stratified-bootstrap CIs; report IQM $+$ CI, never single-seed points.
\item \textbf{Read the ladder, and attribute the hardness.} If a rung is
  \textbf{hard-but-learnable}, do not stop at ``it's hard.'' Verify the hardness is
  attributable to $S$ \emph{specifically}, not to a confound such as an optimisation pathology
  or raw sensory deprivation. A rung can be hard the \emph{wrong} way: a gap measured there
  reflects which architecture escapes the pathology more often, not whether $A$ suits $S$.
  Only an $S$-attributable rung qualifies. If none does, the task \emph{cannot} test the
  hypothesis; report that, with the mechanism.
\end{enumerate}
}}
\end{center}

Two design choices are part of the contribution, not lab habit. \textbf{Pre-registration} of
the ladder and, critically, of a per-outcome \emph{interpretation table} fixed before any run,
prevents the search from degenerating into architecture-fishing: with readings committed in
advance, ``we tried variants until one favoured our architecture'' is structurally impossible,
and every escalation is a dated amendment with a frozen meaning. Pre-registration is long
established in medicine and psychology \citep{chambers2013registered} and has seen dedicated
efforts in machine learning \citep{preregistration2020,preregistration2021}, whose motivation,
that rewarding only positive results suppresses informative negative findings, is closely
aligned with ours. \textbf{The gate/multi-seed split} makes exhaustive laddering affordable: a
single-seed gate is one training run, so many candidate rungs are screened for the price of
one claim-grade cell. Together they turn ``our architecture idea didn't win'' into a
falsifiable, cheap diagnostic about the benchmark. Our own $p=0.8$ rung is the cautionary
case: hard-but-not-ceiling, yet hard the \emph{wrong} way, so it is disqualified by step~5
rather than by its difficulty.

\subsection{A positive control: the protocol returns \emph{yes} where the precondition holds}
\label{sec:poscontrol}

A procedure that has returned only its negative branch invites a fair objection: detector, or
rejection stamp? We run it where the precondition can be inserted and removed at will,
recovering a \emph{known-true} effect: $A=$ convolution, $S=$ spatial locality. The ladder
applies a fixed permutation to a fraction $q$ of MNIST pixel positions. Every rung is a
\textbf{bijection}, a fixed relabeling of input units, so information is provably preserved
and only locality is destroyed as $q\to1$. We compare a small CNN against a parameter-matched
MLP (105.9k parameters each), 10 seeds, at a sub-asymptotic budget. Predictions and
interpretation table were pre-registered.

The CNN$-$MLP gap shrank monotonically from $+2.15$ points at $q=0$, with disjoint 95\% CIs,
to $-2.52$ at $q=1$ (full dose-response in Appendix~\ref{app:poscontrol}). The protocol
therefore reports that the task \emph{can} test $A$-suits-$S$ at $q=0$ and \emph{cannot} at
$q=1$, tracking the effect as it appears and vanishes. \textbf{One pre-registered prediction
missed, informatively, and we report it as such.} We had frozen ``gap $\approx0$ at $q=1$'';
the observed $-2.52$, with disjoint CIs, says the convolutional prior is not merely
uninformative on non-local inputs but mildly \emph{harmful}, since the CNN is constrained to
seek local structure the scrambled task no longer contains while the position-agnostic MLP is
unencumbered. This does not weaken the control; the effect still appears and vanishes with
$S$. But a prediction missed, and we let it stand rather than round it to the number we
pre-registered. A researcher who evaluated only $q=1$ would not see a tie: they would see the
CNN lose by two and a half points and conclude that convolution is harmful for image
classification, a confident wrong answer from a sound multi-seed measurement of a meaningless
comparison. That is the structural position of anyone concluding ``asymmetry hurts in RL''
from our navigation null. The difference is only that here we can \emph{see} the error,
because the same protocol run one rung over recovers the effect in full.

\section{Designing a task that can test the hypothesis}
\label{sec:need}

The null is generative: the analysis that shows this task cannot test the hypothesis also
specifies what a task that \emph{could} would look like. We state it as a property list a
future benchmark can be built against, rather than a vague call for harder tasks.
\textbf{(i)~Genuine, unroutable partial observability.} Hidden state that cannot be recovered
from any single observation \emph{and} cannot be memorised away, which means procedurally
generated or changing layouts, so no fixed-map shortcut exists and the optimal policy must
integrate history every episode. Procedurally generated suites already supply this in
controlled form \citep{cobbe2020procgen,chevalier2023minigrid}.
\textbf{(ii)~Non-redundant observation.} Each policy-relevant distinction encoded once, so a
targeted removal actually removes it rather than leaving parallel channels to carry the load.
\textbf{(iii)~A longer horizon with real credit assignment.} Enough temporal depth that the
optimal policy must compose sub-decisions, making its representation a compositional problem
rather than a one-step lookup. \textbf{(iv)~A policy that is a sharp function even under full
information.} Hardness intrinsic to the optimal \emph{mapping}, with high-frequency structure
and many decision boundaries, rather than induced by hiding information, so that policy
capacity is taxed while value stays comparatively smooth. That last property is precisely the
asymmetry the hypothesis is about, and precisely what the present task lacks. A task meeting
this specification would place the precondition inside a learnable regime, and the comparison
could be run where it is meaningful. Whether the inverted-asymmetry advantage then appears is
an open question this paper does not prejudge.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The transferable lesson is a discipline, not a result: \textbf{before claiming a task confirms
or refutes an architecture hypothesis, test whether the task is able to falsify it.} A cheap,
pre-registered manipulation ladder with single-seed learnability gates makes the check
affordable, and its outcome tells you where, or whether, to spend a claim-grade comparison. In
the terms of \S\ref{sec:related}, this operationalises the construct-validity question as a
prospective, pass/fail experiment run \emph{before} a comparison rather than a retrospective
critique delivered after it. Our negative result is deliberately narrow. We have shown that
this task, and by the mechanism of Appendix~\ref{app:mechanism} static single-map gridworld
navigation with a redundant, low-dimensional observation, cannot test the inverted-asymmetry
hypothesis. We have \textbf{not} shown that inverted asymmetry fails in reinforcement learning
generally; it may well hold on a task of the kind specified above. A single-seed positive on
such a task, our own starting point, is exactly what the protocol is designed to catch.

